RISC-V: load an INT with lwu where its user ignores the upper half, and narrow loads that only feed a
small cast or a byte/halfword mask (applies after perf-43).

An INT is sign-extended in its register, so every INT load is an lw. ZisK proves lw as a load plus a
signextend_w operation in the BinaryExtension state machine, and lwu as a plain load. Lowering now
marks (GTF_ZEXT_LOAD) the GT_IND, GT_LCL_VAR and GT_LCL_FLD INT loads whose single user reads the
same result from either extension, and codegen loads them with lwu:
- stores of 4 bytes or fewer, addw/subw/mulw/negw (not overflow-checked), sllw/srlw/sraw and any
  shift count, casts to a small type, AND with a value in [0, INT32_MAX];
- EQ/NE and unsigned compares (relops and JCMP) against a value in [0, INT32_MAX];
- the length of a bounds check (a zero-extended length can only fail more indices, and only a
  negative length, which no array, string or span has), or its index when the length is in
  [0, INT32_MAX].
LowerArrLength marks every array and string length whatever its user: it lies in [0, INT32_MAX],
where both loads give the same register. A spill of a marked load reloads it with lw, which its user
accepts. The pass runs last in lowering; none of these users asks IsInt32SignExtendedInReg about the
operand, so nothing relies on the sign extension a marked load no longer provides.

A cast to a small type of an INT load, or the load AND 0xFF / 0xFFFF, becomes a narrower load at the
same address (little-endian).

Nethermind stateless guest on ZisK (ziskemu 1.3.0 -X), block 26055159 / 26112793:
  signextend_w 1,020,341 -> 569,875 on the light block; steps unchanged;
  cost 9,342,293,548 -> 9,317,067,579 (-0.27%) / 19,294,339,711 -> 19,246,428,030 (-0.25%).
Outputs identical on 11 mainnet blocks.

